From 1a5edbb9f71fe791a379414a4746818971213463 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The browser Authors <noreply@browser.invalid>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:14:59 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 37/61] linux-fonts

A Linux build presenting Windows (see the user-agent commit) builds Chrome's
fontconfig from the Windows fonts it ships and nothing else, as a Windows
machine has none of Linux's fonts: the files a provider gives
(gfx::SetFontconfigFontFilesProvider) or, without one, the default set in
browser_fonts/base_win11 and emoji_v151 beside the binary, with Windows'
generic families and grayscale, slightly hinted rendering. The configuration
is made current before anything uses fontconfig, so the font service the
renderers use reads it too. Without the shipped fonts, or with
--use-chromium-defaults, the host's fonts stay. Such a build also falls back
as Windows does (font_cache_skia_win.cc): the specified font at standard
style, then Blink's Windows table (font_fallback_win.cc, compiled on Linux),
then fontconfig over the same fonts; and system-ui is Windows' system font,
Segoe UI, unless --system-font-family names one. Its default font
preferences are the ones Windows registers (locale_settings_win.grd:
Consolas as the fixed font, the script fonts for Japanese, Korean, Chinese
and Devanagari, and Windows' Arabic, Cyrillic and Greek entries). Its
ascent, descent and line gap are DirectWrite's (OS/2's Windows values, or
its typographic ones under USE_TYPO_METRICS, and GDI's external leading),
and its average character width is the width of 'x', as Blink takes it when
DirectWrite reports none. Fonts answer to the family names DirectWrite lists
(MS Sans Serif, MS Serif, Franklin Gothic, Segoe UI Variable Display, Text
and Small, Sitka Banner, Display, Subheading, Heading, Small and Text; none
of the Semilight names), and a family name that ends in a weight or a
stretch word ("Segoe UI Light", "Bahnschrift Condensed") resolves as the
base family at that weight or stretch, as font_cache_skia_win.cc resolves
it. Where a face carries an embedded bitmap strike at the size, its advances
are whole pixels, as DirectWrite measures such a face in GDI's classic mode
(Cambria's space at 12 and 16 px). Text is blitted with Windows' gamma and
contrast (sRGB, 1.0; Linux's are 1.2 and 0.2), so the shipped fonts are not
drawn the way anti-detect products on Linux draw them, which Fingerprint
names on sight; --text-contrast and --text-gamma still override. Glyph ink
bounds come from the outline hinted by the font's own instructions, rounded
out to whole pixels, as DirectWrite's texture bounds are, not from the
light-hinted outline FreeType draws. The default font set is added file by
file, as a persona's is, so the family-name rules reach it on every scan,
whatever fontconfig's cache holds from an older build. The configuration has
no aliases for generic names (a page naming "sans-serif" or "Mono" as a
family gets the default font, as on Windows; the unquoted generics resolve
through the preferences), and the advances Skia's FreeType port reports are
scaled by the exact font size rather than the 1/64 px size FreeType was
given, as DirectWrite's are, so text at 13.3333 px measures as on Windows.
Glyphs are drawn as DirectWrite's natural mode draws them wherever
DirectWrite would use that mode (a gasp range without symmetric smoothing,
or up to 20 px on a face with instructions): the font's instructions run as
that mode runs them, and the antialiasing goes across the line only, in 18
steps. Linux draws text with Skia's Fontations port, which a patch to
third_party/skia (fontations-natural-mode, skia/ in the export) teaches the
mode; the first FontCache of each process switches it on.
